00:10:55.000 --> 00:11:12.000
Whitney Hodges: Okay, so just want to share a little bit more with you about Google Ads versus Meta Ads, really kind of how they work, how they compare with organic. How familiar are you with these ad platforms, if at all, Alina? Do you run Google or Meta Ads for your other businesses?

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:17.000
Alina: I do not currently, but I'm in the process of working on all of that.

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:34.000
Whitney Hodges: Okay, awesome. Okay, so on the Google Ads side, so what we like to set up are Google search campaigns, which, you know, gets your, um, you know, your business showing up at the top of the search results, but it's, um, pay-to-play, right, versus.

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:50.000
Whitney Hodges: um, organic, um, SEO, which I'm sure, you know, Amy and her team are all working on for you on the website. So, it's all driven by the keywords that are relevant to, you know, the venue and the services that you provide, right? So, we're bidding on the keywords.

00:11:50.000 --> 00:12:06.000
Whitney Hodges: that we want the venue to show up for, and then your ads are going to be shown for the people who are searching for relevant terms, um, but Google, you know, knows so much about us as users as well, that it is also, in turn, going to show the ad to.

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:23.000
Whitney Hodges: the users with the highest likelihood of needing your services. The Google Ads are pay-per-click, so you're billed on your ad spend based upon if people are actually clicking the ad and then going over to the website or the landing page.

00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:40.000
Whitney Hodges: So to build a really strong Google ad campaign for you. You know, there's a lot of focus on writing strong headlines and add text that really kind of like mirror and play into the keywords while also being in alignment with.

00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:58.000
Whitney Hodges: um, you know, the messaging that's on your website and, um, and landing page, right? Because we want everything to feel aligned. What people see in the ad needs to mirror what they're going to see on the landing page, and that's what's going to lead to, um, not only inquiries, but, you know, aligned inquiries, quality inquiries there.

00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:00.000
Alina: Okay.

00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:16.000
Whitney Hodges: On the, you know, Meta ad side, so these are going to be ads that are running on both Facebook and Instagram. And for Meta, the way that the billing works is different than Google, right?

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:37.000
Whitney Hodges: you're only being charged ad spend if somebody actually clicks on your ad on Meta. If people are seeing your ad, you know you're being charged ad spend accordingly. It's it's you're paying per impression. So on Meta, it's even more important to make sure that you know the messaging, the copy, the creatives are really dialed in and speaking to.

00:13:37.000 --> 00:13:53.000
Whitney Hodges: the right people so that we can not only turn those impressions into clicks, but actual inquiries. And again, you know, aligned inquiries. So for building really strong meta campaigns, you know, we're going to be leveraging the laser focused.

00:13:53.000 --> 00:14:11.000
Whitney Hodges: targeting on the platform, custom audience retargeting, which means, you know, getting your ads in front of people who have visited the website before or have engaged with you on social media, your, you know, your warmer audience members. We often find that incorporating, you know, video content or just, you know, really strong creatives.

00:14:11.000 --> 00:14:17.000
Whitney Hodges: Um, is going to be the key to, you know, cost-effective campaigns that are generating results.

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:19.000
Alina: Okay.

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:38.000
Whitney Hodges: And we love paid ads on these platforms because you're no longer just, you know, relying on the organic algorithms. You really do kind of get to take more control. With SEO, it is very much a long game strategy.

00:14:38.000 --> 00:14:53.000
Whitney Hodges: Um, and with organic SEO, you actually don't really get to control how your search listing shows up in the results. Google will use kind of the information that's on your website or some of your SEO metadata to just kind of decide what the headline looks like, what the text looks like.

00:14:53.000 --> 00:15:09.000
Whitney Hodges: Versus with Google Ads, we get to crack that from scratch, right, and tell Google exactly how we'd like for you to show up. And then on the Meta Ads side of things, it really becomes, you know, a guarantee that people are going to see you in.

00:15:09.000 --> 00:15:30.000
Whitney Hodges: their newsfeed and in their stories, and not just anyone, you know, not just peers or random followers, but, you know, people who we know are engaged, are planning a wedding, or people we're retargeting, right, that have visited your website or engaged with you on social media before.

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:47.000
Whitney Hodges: seen, uh, you know, another ad of yours. So, by leveraging both of these platforms together, we can create a funnel of ads, right? So, different types of content that are meant to be seen by completely cold audiences who have never heard of Dogwood Preserve before.

00:15:47.000 --> 00:16:04.000
Whitney Hodges: versus those warmer retargeting audiences that I've mentioned that may be quite a bit more familiar with the property. And for them the type of content that we want to show them goes quite a bit deeper. Right? We're overcoming objections. We're answering frequently asked questions. We're showing more kind of about the.

00:16:04.000 --> 00:16:20.000
Whitney Hodges: the process. So that's how everything can kind of, you know, work together. Somebody might find you through a Google search or a Google ad, but not be ready to inquire at that point, not be ready to take action. So that's where the Meta ads can then come into play right? Because.

00:16:20.000 --> 00:16:37.000
Whitney Hodges: through the pixel data, we understand, or the Meta paid algorithm is going to understand, who has visited the website before, and then when they open up their Facebook, open up their Instagram, then they can be shown an ad from the venue. So it all kind of creates this really cool.

00:16:37.000 --> 00:16:39.000
Whitney Hodges: ecosystem that works together.

00:16:44.000 --> 00:17:02.000
Whitney Hodges: So, just a visual here of, you know, a funnel and kind of how we're funneling the leads right through your ads. So, um, Google Ads will definitely have the potential to reach cold audiences. We'll set up meta client attraction campaigns that do the exact same thing, that get brand new eyes on the venue.

00:17:02.000 --> 00:17:18.000
Whitney Hodges: um, you know, every… every day, versus, um, content on Meta that's more meant to, you know, really nurture leads and, um, you know, convert them into, uh, inquiries and… and booked tours, of course. Um, and then, of course, you know, you have your.

00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:28.000
Whitney Hodges: your, uh, your lead lock funnel as well. People who are actually inquiring and then getting nurtured through the system, being converted into tours, all of that good stuff.

00:17:28.000 --> 00:17:29.000
Alina: Cool.

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:48.000
Whitney Hodges: So, um, ads don't work in isolation. You know, they are one piece of the overall marketing, um, that you have in play, right? So just one kind of piece of the puzzle. So that's why I like to just kind of go over with you, um, the foundations that we see for success with.

00:17:48.000 --> 00:17:50.000
Whitney Hodges: digital ads.

00:17:52.000 --> 00:18:09.000
Whitney Hodges: Uh, in our opinion, it really is all about alignment, and alignment in five different areas. Um, so brand alignment, website and landing page alignment, sales funnel alignment, data alignment, and then mindset alignment. So let's get into each of these a little bit.

00:18:09.000 --> 00:18:24.000
Whitney Hodges: Um, the first one, brand alignment. So, um, really kind of gist of this is that, like I said, ads don't work in isolation, right? You want to be doing things to organically market the business as well, which I know that you.

00:18:24.000 --> 00:18:41.000
Whitney Hodges: are and you know, I can already tell, just kind of based on the way that you filled out your your onboarding questionnaire and the the website. That you do have a good, you know, understanding, and are able to articulate. You know your your unique selling proposition or unique value proposition.

00:18:41.000 --> 00:19:00.000
Whitney Hodges: What we see is that the organic and paid algorithms really do complement one another, right? So it's not just a matter of just focusing on paid ads on Google and on Meta, but it's also, you know, the SEO on Google, the, you know, organic posting on Facebook and on Instagram.

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:17.000
Whitney Hodges: Paid ads are going to increase your visibility across the board, across the entire digital ecosystem. So this can be a good opportunity to just kind of take a look at, you know, what you're doing on Facebook, on Instagram in particular, or, you know, your Google business profile, and just really kind of make sure that you're putting.

00:19:17.000 --> 00:19:26.000
Whitney Hodges: your best foot forward there, because, like I said, you're definitely going to be getting more visibility across the board when these ads start running.

00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:31.000
Alina: Yeah, one of the things we've got to figure out how to fix, and I don't know…

00:19:31.000 --> 00:19:48.000
Alina: what the broken link is in regards to, but the Facebook, I cannot figure out how to get, um, our Facebook connected. It's… I don't know what's happened. I don't know if it's because we had to change the name, but it has been a pain in the…

00:19:45.000 --> 00:19:47.000
Whitney Hodges: Mmm.

00:19:48.000 --> 00:19:50.000
Alina: Booty.

00:19:50.000 --> 00:20:05.000
Whitney Hodges: Okay, okay, good to know. We'll definitely see if we can kind of take a look at that today and see what the issue may be there. And happy to help with troubleshooting that any way that I can, even if it, you know, requires getting.

00:20:05.000 --> 00:20:06.000
Whitney Hodges: Um, so…

00:20:06.000 --> 00:20:11.000
Whitney Hodges: Meta or Facebook like help or support involved sort of thing.

00:20:10.000 --> 00:20:11.000
Alina: Okay.

00:20:11.000 --> 00:20:13.000
Alina: That'd be great.

00:20:16.000 --> 00:20:34.000
Whitney Hodges: Okay, so the second piece of it, making sure that, you know, your website and or landing page are really aligned and are ready for paid traffic. So, we do recommend creating a dedicated landing page for your ads traffic, because.

00:20:34.000 --> 00:20:52.000
Whitney Hodges: What this will do, as opposed to sending people to, you know, the website as a whole, is it can give them one clear next step of what action that we want them to take, which essentially is to, you know, inquire to enter that lead lock funnel so that they're then leads for you that can be nurtured and become.

00:20:52.000 --> 00:20:58.000
Whitney Hodges: um, tours. We find that, you know, when we send traffic to, um.

00:20:58.000 --> 00:21:16.000
Whitney Hodges: you know, the homepage of a website, it can work, it can convert, but there are also a lot of different, uh, distractions, almost, right? Different paths that people can take of different pages to check out, or galleries to view, or blogs to read, um, things like that. So, um, that's why we do like to focus on, you know, an ads traffic landing page.

00:21:16.000 --> 00:21:24.000
Whitney Hodges: a clear conversion-focused experience that's mobile-friendly, that loads quickly, um, with, you know, an easily accessible.

00:21:25.000 --> 00:21:42.000
Whitney Hodges: So, um, so right now, I think you just have the, the website, right? So part of our process, um, is creating an ads traffic landing page for you in LeadLock, and, and that's included in the scope. Um, but I know, Tirza, that you and I also kind of.

